Mild superficial tinea pedis (athlete's foot) presenting with interdigital maceration and itch
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Interdigital pruritus, White macerated epidermis, Scaling of the toe webs

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-025
Reason for Visit: Severe foot pain, intense itching, and epidermal breakdown requiring assessment to rule out superimposed bacterial cellulitis or diabetic foot ulceration.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe localized foot pain and skin breakdown. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to rule out spreading bacterial cellulitis originating from the interdigital fissures, ensuring limb-threatening infections were identified and treated immediately.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The skin between my toes is peeling off and the itching is absolutely unbearable when I take my work boots off.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes intensely pruritic interdigital spaces most pronounced between the fourth and fifth toes, evolving over the past week. Reports white macerated and soggy epidermis localized in the toe webs, leading to superficial epidermal scaling and peeling. Notes an odorous localized desquamation. Exacerbation is clearly correlated with wearing occlusive footwear and excessive sweating at work. Denies fevers, chills, or spreading red streaks up the leg.
Functional Status: Fully independent, ambulation mildly uncomfortable.
Social/Safety Concerns: Heavy occlusive footwear required for daily occupation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/80 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 84 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, focused on the localized pedal discomfort.
Physical Exam: Foot examination reveals diagnostic triad: intense interdigital pruritus, white macerated epidermis, and scaling of the toe webs. Mild underlying demarcated skin erythema is present, strictly confined to the web spaces. Intact toenails without structural dystrophy or thickening. Complete absence of spreading cellulitis on the dorsum of the foot or ascending lymphangitis. Normal distal pedal pulses and brisk capillary refill. Sensation to light touch is intact.
Environmental Observations: Heavy, non-breathable leather work boots at the entryway.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient has applied over-the-counter moisturizer, worsening the maceration.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild superficial tinea pedis (athlete's foot) presenting with interdigital maceration and itch - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Lower Limb Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Erythema is strictly localized to the toe webs, complete absence of spreading warmth, induration, or systemic fever.

Interdigital Erythrasma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Typical intense scaling and maceration rather than a dry, shiny brown/red plaque (would lack coral-red fluorescence under Wood's lamp).

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lesions restricted purely to the moist web spaces rather than the dorsum of the foot or areas of direct shoe contact.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute skin breakdown and severe pruritus on a weight-bearing extremity. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandated to rule out an occult bacterial cellulitis entering through the compromised epidermal barrier, which could rapidly progress if untreated. The physical examination confirmed intact neurovascular status and restricted the pathology to a superficial dermatophyte infection, safely averting emergency department presentation and inappropriate systemic antibiotic use.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescription for topical antifungal cream (e.g., terbinafine 1%) to be applied once or twice daily for 1-2 weeks. Instruction to immediately cease applying moisturizing lotions between the toes.
Positive Behavioral Change: Dry thoroughly between the toes with a separate, designated towel or hairdryer immediately after showering, preventing the moist environment fungi need to thrive, ensuring the feet feel completely dry before putting on socks.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Moisture-wicking merino wool or synthetic blend socks.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the fissures become painful, hot, or begin to discharge pus.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if red streaks begin traveling up your foot and leg, if the foot becomes entirely swollen and hot, or if you develop a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; recurrent fungal infections require strict environmental management of footwear.
Clinical Course: Itching typically improves within 3 to 5 days of starting the antifungal cream, with complete skin healing taking up to 2 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the cracks between your toes start oozing yellow pus, or if the redness spreads to the top of your foot.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A fungus that thrives in dark, warm, moist environments has infected the dead skin between your toes. The antifungal cream kills the fungus, but keeping the area bone-dry is required to prevent it from returning.
Actionable Steps: Apply the cream exactly as directed, extending 2 cm beyond the visible rash. Change your socks twice a day if your feet sweat. Never put moisturizer between your toes.

